\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Every ratio $r$ below is the paired width ratio defined in \cref{sec:experiments}, and ``label saving'' is $1-r^2$. ``PPI++'' is the package's, ``the strongest valid baseline'' is as defined there, and ``the selected interval'' is the headline arm. Coverage is nominal 95\%, from at least 2000 repetitions per cell (5000 for $n\le100$).

\paragraph{Real data: no interval we build is more than about 1\% shorter than the strongest PPI++.}
At the reference $n$ the selected interval has $r$ between $0.994$ and $1.008$ against the strongest valid baseline on the nine datasets, and all nine are classified \emph{no gain} (\cref{fig:ladder}, \cref{tab:main}). Against the package's PPI++ the range is $0.971$ to $1.007$ and eight are no gain; \texttt{gene\_expression} ($r=0.971$, label saving $5.7\%$) is undecided, for the reason given below. In measured widths the estimated floor is between $0.967$ and $1.010$ times the package's interval, so in the model classes we fit no interval built from the same labels, unlabeled records and $f$ is more than a few percent shorter than PPI++ at that $n$. This is an estimate within a class, not a bound: seven cross-fitted classes (splines, isotonic, strata means, boosting) change $\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}$ by less than $0.2\%$, but a richer class could find more. Over the $51$ dataset--$n$ cells ($n$ from 30 to 3000), against the package $38$ are no gain, $12$ undecided and one is a gain (\texttt{gene\_expression}, $n=100$, $r=0.930$, saving $13\%$); against the strongest valid baseline $39$ are no gain, $12$ undecided and none is a gain. In five of the $13$ headline cells (\texttt{alphafold}, \texttt{census\_income} and \texttt{forest} with $f$ alone; \texttt{alphafold} and \texttt{census\_income} with side information) neither PPI++ passes the coverage rule at the reference $n$ (marked $\ddagger$); all five are no gain ($r=1.000$ to $1.009$ against the narrower of the two) and are read with that caveat. The labels-only interval is $1.15$ to $2.9$ times as wide as PPI++: the use of $f$ matters far more than what remains.

