\paragraph{The one gain over the package comes from its clip, not from calibration.}
On \texttt{gene\_expression} the selected interval has $r=0.917$, $0.930$, $0.954$ and $0.971$ against the package's PPI++ at $n=30$, $100$, $300$ and $500$ (a gain by the pre-fixed rule at $n=100$), but $0.927$, $0.974$, $0.992$ and $0.994$ against the unclipped PPI++: undecided at $n=100$ and no gain from $n=300$. The slope of $Y$ on $f$ is $b^\star=1.15$ there, $1.38$ on \texttt{census\_income} and marginally above one on \texttt{alphafold} ($1.03$; the formula predicts a clip effect under $0.1\%$), and below one on the other six. Where $b^\star>1$ the package's clip binds ($\hat\lambda>1$ in $71\%$ of \texttt{gene\_expression} draws at $n=30$) and the unclipped interval is narrower, as \eqref{eq:clip} says: by $4.5\%$ at $n=100$ and $2.3\%$ at $n=500$ on \texttt{gene\_expression} ($2.4\%$ and $1.5\%$ on \texttt{census\_income}), the formula being within $0.031$ of the measured ratio for $n\ge100$. In a synthetic sweep with an affine $\E[Y\mid f]$ it agrees within $0.014$ for $n\ge300$ (\cref{app:proofs}). It is not learning $g$: at $n=100$ the standard deviation of the estimate relative to the package's is $0.890$ for PPI++ with the population-optimal weight, $0.896$ for the pooled linear estimator and $0.921$ for the cross-fitted spline, and the selected interval picks the line in $95\%$ of draws. Of the $7$ points by which the pooled linear estimator is narrower ($r=0.933$), most come from the clip and about $2.5$ from the package's slightly small standard error (SE over SD $0.974$, against $1.00$ to $1.015$ for the others). The unclipped interval is not uniformly narrower: clipping shrinks a noisy $\hat\lambda$ at small $n$ (\texttt{alphafold}, $n=30$: the unclipped interval is $7\%$ wider, the cost of estimating $\lambda$), and on \texttt{census\_income} its coverage is $0.79$ to $0.92$ at $n\le300$, valid only from $n=1000$. The result concerns one implementation of PPI++, not the method, and we claim no gain from calibration on any real dataset.

\paragraph{Where there is room, and what it costs to take it.}
The selected interval gains a lot when $\E[Y\mid f]$ is far from affine. In the design $Y=0.8f+c(f^2-1)+0.6\varepsilon$ with $f\sim N(0,1)$ (so $\Delta=2c^2$), $r$ at $n=1600$ is $0.73$ for $c=0.5$ (saving $47\%$), $0.93$ for $c=0.2$ and $0.997$ for $c=0.05$; with a saturating or a non-monotone calibration it is $0.85$ and $0.81$, and with an exactly affine $\E[Y\mid f]$ it is $1.001$, as \cref{cor:noroom} requires. The excess variance of PPI++ over the floor, $(1/n-1/M)\Delta$ (\cref{eq:gap}), is the leading term: against the oracle that uses the true $\E[Y\mid f]$, on the same draws, the measured-to-predicted ratio lies between $0.86$ and $1.14$ at every $n$ from 25 to 1600 for the designs with $\Delta\ge0.23$ (right panel of \cref{fig:breakeven}); for small $\Delta$ it is larger at small $n$ (ratio $11$ at $n=25$ for $c=0.05$), the cost of estimating $\lambda$ that \eqref{eq:gap} omits.

\Cref{fig:breakeven} shows the price. At small $n$ the calibrated arms are wider than PPI++ (up to $r=1.21$ at $n=25$ for spline calibration with $c=0.05$) and cross below $r=1$ near the predicted break-even $n^\ast$ of \eqref{eq:nstar}. We score the prediction only where the grid brackets the crossing, within the factor of three fixed in advance; the other curves are censored and do not confirm the formula (\cref{tab:scoring} lists every population). Of the $10$ bracketed synthetic and transformed-real curves, $9$ are within the factor; the miss is \texttt{census\_income} with a log-transformed predictor (predicted $42$, crossing between $300$ and $500$). On the nine real datasets eight are censored: the predicted $n^\ast$ lies above the largest $n$ we ran for six (for example $2\times10^4$ for \texttt{galaxies}), and is about $2\times10^3$ for \texttt{alphafold} and \texttt{census\_income}, where the spline arm records no win. \texttt{gene\_expression} is bracketed and a miss: $n^\ast$ is predicted as $355$ and the calibrated arm first beats both PPI++ intervals between $n=30$ and $100$, a crossing that follows the slope $b^\star>1$ rather than the learning-cost trade-off (at $n=100$ the diagnostic puts $\hat\Delta$ at $0.022$, a predicted gain of $-2.5\%$). So \eqref{eq:nstar} holds where it can be tested on synthetic and transformed-real populations, cannot be tested on eight of nine real datasets, and fails on \texttt{gene\_expression} for a stated reason and on one other case for a reason we have not tested.

\paragraph{Price of learning and validity.}
At $n\ge300$ the selected interval is never wider than the strongest valid baseline by more than $1.2\%$ (median $r=1.001$) and covers between $0.925$ and $0.958$; at the reference $n$ its coverage is $0.939$ to $0.955$ (\cref{tab:main}). At $n\le100$ it is wider: median $r=1.019$ over the $18$ cells (range $0.927$ to $1.116$), and $1.055$ at $n=30$. This is a cost of learning, not a loss of validity: the Wald interval of PPI++ itself undercovers at small $n$ ($0.880$ to $0.943$ at $n=100$ on the real datasets), the selected interval has median coverage $0.927$ at $n\le100$, and in $16$ of the $18$ cells it covers no worse than PPI++. A ratio below one at these sizes can be an undercoverage artefact (\texttt{ballots}, $n=30$: $r=0.93$, coverage $0.806$), counted as invalid, not as a gain.

\paragraph{Where every method fails.}
\Cref{fig:regime} marks the cells where nothing is valid. Under a distribution shift (\texttt{plankton}, labels from 2013, unlabeled records from 2014, truth the 2014 mean) PPI++ covers $0.94$ at $n=200$ and $1000$ and $0.09$ at $n=5000$, where its bias of $0.012$ exceeds its width of $0.013$. When labeling is selected on the data (probability increasing in $f$, $X$ or $Y$) the PPI++ interval covers $0$ in seven of the nine designs, $0.80$ in one and $0.94$ in one, and the selected interval, shorter in eight of them, covers between $0$ and $0.67$: this violates MCAR (\cref{sec:setting}), and a shorter interval is no improvement. For binary $Y$ at 2\% to 5\% prevalence, Wald intervals undercover at $n\le300$ for every method.

\paragraph{Interval construction, side information, and when headroom is visible.}
\label{sec:sidenull}
For binary outcomes at $n\le200$, Wilson, $t$ and truncated Wald intervals change the coverage of PPI++ by at most five points and none restores nominal coverage at the smallest sizes; bootstrap-$t$ is no better and up to $50\%$ wider (\cref{app:constructions}). On the census tasks and \texttt{alphafold}, gradient-boosting AIPW at $n=200$ and $1000$ is wider than PPI++ ($r=1.02$ to $1.11$) and the selected T2 interval is at most $0.1\%$ shorter (\cref{app:sidex}). At the reference $n=500$ it has $r=1.009$, $1.004$ and $1.004$ on \texttt{census\_education}, \texttt{census\_healthcare} and \texttt{alphafold}, and on \texttt{census\_income} $0.993$ against the package's PPI++ (the clip again) and $1.009$ against the unclipped one; all four are no gain. A positive control shows that the pipeline detects gains when they exist: where $X$ carries a linear signal beyond $f$ the selected interval has $r=0.81$ to $0.90$, and where the signal is nonlinear only $0.88$ and $0.86$ at $n=400$ and $1600$. The diagnostic of \cref{app:diagnostic}, the cross-fitted estimate of $\Delta$ minus the learning cost computed on the labels alone, has the same sign as the realised gain in $94\%$ of $129$ dataset--$n$ cells (Spearman correlation $0.94$), so the labels alone indicate whether the calibrated alternative is worth running.
